  "full_response": "**Eventual Consistency** is a consistency model used in distributed systems to achieve high availability and partition tolerance (as per the **CAP Theorem**). It guarantees that if no new updates are made to a given data item, eventually all accesses to that item will return the last updated value. However, it does not provide immediate consistency—there may be a period where different nodes in the system have different versions of the data.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Concepts of Eventual Consistency**\n1. **No Immediate Guarantees**\n   - Unlike **strong consistency** (where all reads return the most recent write), eventual consistency allows temporary inconsistencies.\n   - Example: If you update a user's profile picture, some users might see the old picture for a short time before the update propagates.\n\n2. **Convergence Over Time**\n   - The system ensures that, given enough time without new updates, all replicas will eventually reflect the same value.\n   - This is achieved through mechanisms like **replication**, **anti-entropy protocols**, or **gossip protocols**.\n\n3. **Trade-off for Performance & Availability**\n   - Eventual consistency is often used in systems where **low latency** and **high availability** are prioritized over immediate consistency (e.g., social media feeds, DNS, CDNs).\n   - It allows the system to remain operational even during network partitions (unlike strong consistency, which may block operations).\n\n---\n\n### **When is Eventual Consistency Used?**\n- **Large-scale distributed databases** (e.g., Cassandra, DynamoDB, Riak).\n- **Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)** (e.g., caching systems where stale data is acceptable for a short time).\n- **Social media platforms** (e.g., Facebook, Twitter, where slight delays in updates are tolerable).\n- **Multi-region cloud services** (where network latency makes strong consistency impractical).\n\n---\n\n### **Examples of Eventual Consistency**\n1. **DNS (Domain Name System)**\n   - When you update a DNS record, it may take minutes or hours for all DNS servers worldwide to reflect the change.\n\n2. **Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service)**\n   - If you upload a file, it may not be immediately visible to all users due to replication delays.\n\n3. **Social Media Feeds**\n   - When you post a tweet, it may not appear instantly for all followers due to replication across data centers.\n\n---\n\n### **How is Eventual Consistency Achieved?**\nSeveral techniques help enforce",
